---
// /holdings/neighbors — the field: every experiment, and what already exists near it.
//
// Built 2026-08-09 from the parked package of the USP rework program
// (docs/design/2026-08-09-usp-rework-program.md). Phase 0 put a "nearest neighbours" section on
// every experiment page; this page is the same honesty read at once, as one drawing, so a
// visitor can see the shelf's exposure without opening sixteen pages.
//
// The USP obligation applies to this page too, and its answer is the "where this stands"
// section below: the neighbours of the FIGURE were searched before it was built.
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'
import NeighborhoodFigure from '@/components/holdings/NeighborhoodFigure.astro'
import { GRAPH } from '@/lib/graph/load'
import { neighborhoodField } from '@/lib/graph/query'

const entries = neighborhoodField(GRAPH)
const neighbors = entries.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.neighbors.length, 0)
const counted = {
  redundant: entries.filter((e) => e.work.verdict === 'REDUNDANT').length,
  added: entries.filter((e) => e.work.verdict === 'ADDED VALUE').length,
  unique: entries.filter((e) => e.work.verdict === 'UNIQUE').length,
}
---

<Page
  title="The field | Experiments | Frank Bültge"
  description="Every experiment of this lab drawn against the projects that already do something like it — the daylight, or the lack of it, made visible."
>
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl flex-1 px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
      <a href="/holdings" class="hover:text-accent-soft">Experiments</a> · the field
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-[clamp(30px,5vw,52px)] font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]">Where each experiment stands</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-fg-muted">
      On 2026-08-09 every experiment on this shelf was searched for its strongest existing neighbours — projects,
      tools, papers and services that would embarrass its claim rather than flatter it. {neighbors} of them were found
      and named. This is that audit as one drawing: each experiment on the ring, its neighbours inward on the same
      line, and between them the daylight it was granted — <strong>{counted.unique}</strong> wide,
      <strong>{counted.added}</strong> partial, <strong>{counted.redundant}</strong> little.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-fg-faint">
      The verdicts are drafts by the auditing machines, offered for judgement, not decisions — keep, rework and
      archive calls are Frank's, expected alongside the reading of 2026-09-05. Nothing on this page is a measure of
      similarity; it is a record of what a search found.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <NeighborhoodFigure />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-16 border-t border-line/60 pt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Where this figure stands</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        The same obligation this page enforces on the shelf applies to the page. Its nearest neighbours, searched
        before it was built:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 max-w-2xl space-y-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        <li>
          <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://www.connectedpapers.com" rel="noopener nofollow">Connected Papers</a>,
          <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://www.litmaps.com" rel="noopener nofollow">Litmaps</a>,
          <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://openknowledgemaps.org" rel="noopener nofollow">Open Knowledge Maps</a>
          and ResearchRabbit — the literature maps. Same picture: a work in the middle, related work around it. They
          draw <em>other people's</em> papers around a seed, by computed similarity, and they judge nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Patent prior-art landscaping (Questel Orbit, Relecura, IPRally, Ambercite; the USPTO's own
          <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://patents.google.com/patent/US20250251850A1/en" rel="noopener nofollow">interactive prior-art map</a>
          patents) — the closest neighbour on PURPOSE: position a claim against what already exists. Private
          instruments for an applicant, produced to defend a claim, not published against it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397399132" rel="noopener nofollow">“Being Ranked in a Material World”</a>
          and the distinctiveness literature — originality measured as cosine distance from a work to its nearest
          prior works. The closest neighbour on IDEA: daylight as distance. Theirs is computed in an embedding space
          and lives in a paper; this one is a cited verdict with links, standing on the site of the work it judges.
        </li>
        <li>
          The open-source “Alternatives” section (<a class="underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-soft" href="https://www.makeareadme.com" rel="noopener nofollow">Make a README</a>
          recommends listing competitors with the differentiating factors) — the honest genre this belongs to. Prose,
          per project, written by the maker, rarely dated and rarely adversarial.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        <strong class="text-fg">The daylight, named:</strong> none of them is a maker's own portfolio drawn against
        its prior art as a public, versioned, adversarially sourced figure — one where the distance is a dated
        judgement with named links rather than a computed similarity, where the search was instructed to find what
        would embarrass the work, and where the drawing fails its own build if a quotation drifts from the audit it
        came out of. The literature maps map the field; this one is a maker mapping his own exposure, and publishing
        the result at the entrance to the work.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-xs leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
        Searched 2026-08-09 (web search + Tavily) for: prior-art landscape maps, literature-similarity maps,
        originality-by-nearest-neighbour metrics, self-published competitor comparisons. A verdict of “no neighbour
        found” rests on absence of evidence and is worth less than the positive findings above — if you know the
        piece this misses, it belongs on this page.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Page>
